    "examples": [
      {
        "question_text": "The heights, in metres, of six trees are $7.4$, $6.8$, $7.1$, $7.6$, $18.9$ and $7.2$.\nDescribe the cluster, the gap and the outlier.",
        "solution_text": "Order the heights: $6.8,7.1,7.2,7.4,7.6,18.9$.\nFive heights are close together from $6.8$ m to $7.6$ m, so this is the cluster.\nThere is a gap between $7.6$ m and $18.9$ m: no tree has a recorded height in that interval.\nThe outlier is $18.9$ m, much higher than the other five trees."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "The graph shows the daily maximum temperature over six days.\n[tikz]\n% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"clusters-gaps-outliers scaled display example; source-adapted statistical display; ordinary black lines, labels and data points; no shaded region\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.12cm]\n\\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,34);\\draw[->] (0,0)--(6.8,0);\n\\foreach \\v in {0,5,10,15,20,25,30} {\\draw (-0.12,\\v)--(0,\\v);\\node[left] at (-0.15,\\v) {$\\v$};}\n\\foreach \\x in {1,...,6} {\\node[below] at (\\x,0) {$\\x$};}\n\\draw (1,14)--(2,16)--(3,15)--(4,31)--(5,17)--(6,16);\n\\foreach \\x/\\y in {1/14,2/16,3/15,4/31,5/17,6/16} {\\fill (\\x,\\y) circle (1.8pt);\\node[above] at (\\x,\\y) {$\\y$};}\n\\node[rotate=90] at (-1.25,17) {Temperature ($^{\\circ}$C)};\n\\node[below] at (3.5,-3) {Day};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n(a) Read the six temperatures.\n(b) Describe the cluster, the gap and the outlier. State which day had the outlier.",
        "solution_text": "(a) Read the temperature scale, not the day numbers: $14,16,15,31,17,16$ in day order.\n(b) In order: $14,15,16,16,17,31$.\nFive temperatures are close together from $14^{\\circ}$C to $17^{\\circ}$C, forming a cluster.\nThere is a gap between $17^{\\circ}$C and $31^{\\circ}$C, with no recorded temperature in that interval.\nThe outlier is $31^{\\circ}$C on Day $4$, much higher than the other five temperatures."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Each dot shows one tree height. The two plots use the same heights; only the number of trees at $21$ m changes.\nPlot A:\n[tikz]\n% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"clusters-gaps-outliers worked example; source-adapted dot plot; ordinary black lines, labels and data dots; no shaded region\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.48cm,y=0.45cm]\n\\draw[<->] (9.5,0)--(22.5,0);\n\\foreach \\x in {10,...,22} {\\draw (\\x,-0.15)--(\\x,0.15);}\n\\foreach \\x in {11,13,15,17,19,21} {\\node[below] at (\\x,-0.2) {$\\x$};}\n\\foreach \\x/\\c in {11/3,12/4,13/3,21/1} {\\foreach \\k in {1,...,\\c} {\\fill (\\x,\\k) circle (1.8pt);}}\n\\node[below] at (16,-1.2) {Height (m)};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\nPlot B:\n[tikz]\n% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"clusters-gaps-outliers worked example; source-adapted dot plot; ordinary black lines, labels and data dots; no shaded region\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.48cm,y=0.45cm]\n\\draw[<->] (9.5,0)--(22.5,0);\n\\foreach \\x in {10,...,22} {\\draw (\\x,-0.15)--(\\x,0.15);}\n\\foreach \\x in {11,13,15,17,19,21} {\\node[below] at (\\x,-0.2) {$\\x$};}\n\\foreach \\x/\\c in {11/3,12/4,13/3,21/3} {\\foreach \\k in {1,...,\\c} {\\fill (\\x,\\k) circle (1.8pt);}}\n\\node[below] at (16,-1.2) {Height (m)};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]\n(a) Describe the clusters and gap in Plot B.\n(b) Which plot shows a single outlier at $21$ m? Explain.",
        "solution_text": "(a) Plot B has ten heights from $11$ m to $13$ m and three heights at $21$ m. These form two groups.\nThere is a gap between $13$ m and $21$ m, with no heights at the ticks $14$ m to $20$ m.\n(b) Plot A has a single outlier at $21$ m, far above the group from $11$ m to $13$ m.\nIn Plot B, three trees have height $21$ m. They form a second group, rather than a single height standing alone."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Two streets have these house prices, in thousands of dollars:\nStreet A: $\\$43,\\$56,\\$69,\\$82,\\$95,\\$108$\nStreet B: $\\$43,\\$46,\\$48,\\$51,\\$54,\\$108$\nBoth have the same lowest and highest prices. Which street has an outlier? Explain why the highest price is not automatically an outlier.",
        "solution_text": "In Street A the prices rise in equal steps of $\\$13$ thousand. No price sits far from the rest, so there is no outlier.\nIn Street B, five prices are grouped from $\\$43$ thousand to $\\$54$ thousand. The price $\\$108$ thousand is much higher and stands alone, so it is an outlier.\nBoth streets have the same spread, but only Street B has an isolated price. The highest price is an outlier only when it is far from the rest."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Which value is the outlier in $\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{31}{50},\\frac{3}{20},0.57,\\frac{29}{50},\\frac{59}{100}$?",
        "solution_text": "Write the fractions as decimals: $0.60,0.62,0.15,0.57,0.58,0.59$.\nIn order: $0.15,0.57,0.58,0.59,0.60,0.62$.\nFive values cluster from $0.57$ to $0.62$. The outlier is $\\frac{3}{20}=0.15$, much lower than the other five values."
      },
      {
        "question_text": "Identify any clusters and the large gap in this stem-and-leaf plot.\n[tikz]\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={text=black,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont}]\n\\node[anchor=east] at (0,0) {Stem};\n\\node[anchor=west] at (0.4,0) {Leaves};\n\\draw[black] (0.2,0.3)--(0.2,-3.5);\n\\draw[black] (-1,-0.3)--(4.8,-0.3);\n\\foreach \\s/\\y in {3/-0.8,4/-1.4,5/-2.0,6/-2.6,7/-3.2} {\\node[anchor=east] at (0,\\y) {$\\s$};}\n\\node[anchor=west] at (0.4,-0.8) {$2\\quad5\\quad8$};\n\\node[anchor=west] at (0.4,-1.4) {$1\\quad3\\quad6$};\n\\node[anchor=west] at (0.4,-2.6) {$7\\quad8\\quad9$};\n\\node[anchor=west] at (0.4,-3.2) {$0\\quad2$};\n\\node[anchor=west] at (-1,-4.0) {Key: $3\\mid2=32$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n[/tikz]",
        "solution_text": "Use the key to read the values: $32,35,38,41,43,46,67,68,69,70,72$.\nSix values cluster from $32$ to $46$, and five values cluster from $67$ to $72$.\nThe large gap is between $46$ and $67$, with no data values in that interval. The empty stem $5$ shows that there are no values from $50$ to $59$."
      }
    ],
